<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/16:_Chemistry_of_Benzene_-_Electrophilic_Aromatic_Substitution/16.05:_An_Explanation_of_Substituent_Effects" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B4.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-31577" id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B4.7D.5C)-31577"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{4}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-31577">Classify these two groups as activating or deactivating groups:</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1 lt-chem-31577">A. alcohol</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1 lt-chem-31577">B. ester</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-31577">A. alcohol is an activating group.</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-31577">B. Esters can be either. If the oxygen atom is next to the ring, esters are activating. However if the carbonyl is next to the ring, the ester is a deactivating group.</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
</div></section>

<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/16:_Chemistry_of_Benzene_-_Electrophilic_Aromatic_Substitution/16.05:_An_Explanation_of_Substituent_Effects" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B5.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-31577" id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B5.7D.5C)-31577"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{5}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-31577">Does a chloride substituent activate or deactivate an aromatic ring?</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-31577">Chloride deactivate an aromatic ring due to the inductive effect.</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
</div></section>

<section class="box-exercise">
